// A function's fingerprint, whole: one entry per function, chained or not.
//
// A chain function is one unit of logic. A later segment's meaning depends on
// the values the earlier ones computed, so it is not a unit on its own: two
// chain functions are duplicates only when their signatures and every segment,
// in order, match -- the same test a function without a chain gets. A chain
// that merely ends the way another function ends is not a copy of it.
//
// The body is fingerprinted as the NODE LIST, `chain` markers included, which
// canon_body writes as a fixed seam (../../stmt/more/seam.id). Nothing ever
// splits the finished string again, so a `|` inside a string literal -- `|` is
// the field separator in the compiler's own --calls format strings -- cannot
// forge a boundary. Parameters and locals are numbered across the whole
// function, so a local declared in one segment keeps its number in the next.
//
// The signature is part of the fingerprint, so two functions collide only when
// they agree on it as well as on the logic. A `native` has no body to walk and
// is fingerprinted by name instead: ./kind/native.id.
//
// The names here are deliberately unshared: `out`, `seg`, `sig` and `ps`
// already mean other things elsewhere in the tree, and a name keeps one type
// across the whole program (docs/PROJECT.md, checklist 6).

unit_plain(int id) {
  string unit_sigtext = unit_sig(id);
  string unit_fp = unit_walk(id, unit_sigtext);
} return string unit_fp;
